<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Continental Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1774">1774</a>–<a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Continental Congress was the assembly of delegates from the Thirteen Colonies that directed American resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and then governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in 1774; the Second, from 1775, created the Continental Army, adopted the Declaration of Independence on 4 July 1776 and drafted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a>. It continued as the Congress of the Confederation until 1789.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/continental-congress.jpg" alt="Continental Congress" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Édouard Armand-Dumaresq · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signing_of_Declaration_of_Independence_by_Armand-Dumaresq,_c1873_-_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The idea of a joint colonial body first arose in 1754, at the start of the French and Indian War, when representatives of seven of the thirteen colonies met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albany-congress">Albany Congress</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a> proposed a confederation. The proposal was rejected. A further step came with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamp-act-congress">Stamp Act Congress</a>, which met in New York City in October 1765 and sent a Declaration of Rights and Grievances to Parliament. The Stamp Act was repealed in March 1766. The Continental Congress drew its organisation less on Parliament than on that congress, and nine of its delegates sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> met in Carpenter's Hall, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, from 5 September to 26 October 1774. Fifty-six delegates from twelve colonies attended, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrick-henry">Patrick Henry</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a>, with Peyton Randolph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> as president. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> did not take part. The delegates organised an economic boycott of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, adopted the Continental Association and petitioned the King for redress. Most were not yet ready to break away from Britain.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a> convened on 10 May 1775 at the State House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, shortly after fighting began. It raised armies, directed strategy, appointed diplomats and made treaties. On 14 June it established the Continental Army and on 15 June appointed Washington its commander. It sent the Olive Branch Petition to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>, but after the Proclamation of Rebellion of August 1775 some members concluded that peace would not come. It adopted Richard Henry Lee's resolution for independence on 2 July 1776 and, two days later, the Declaration of Independence, drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>. It created standing committees, including a board of war and a navy board, and approved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a> on 15 November 1777.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Articles came into force on 1 March 1781, and the body became the Congress of the Confederation, a single chamber in which each state had one vote. It could declare war, sign treaties and borrow or print money, but it could not tax. It met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and later in other cities, including Annapolis and New York City, and its authority declined after the war. Its Northwest Ordinance of 1787 placed the land north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a> under federal control. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-convention-united-states">Philadelphia Convention</a> of 1787 was authorised to amend the Articles, and the first Congress under the new Constitution replaced the Confederation Congress in 1789.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Continental Congress” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They took the title from the presiding officers of colonial councils and of the <b>Continental Congress</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The title phrase is said to come from Benjamin Franklin, who was very popular in France during his stay as the <b>Continental Congress</b>'s representative from 1776 to 1785 and reportedly answered questions about the American war with &quot;Ça ira, ça ira&quot; in his imperfect French.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ca-ira">Ça Ira</a></li><li><blockquote>Most members of the <b>Continental Congress</b> signed the parchment copy of the Declaration in Philadelphia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-2">2 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Continental Congress</b> set aside 18 December as a day of thanksgiving for the victory at Saratoga.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-18">18 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Continental Congress</b> established an &quot;Army Hospital&quot;, overseen by a Director General and Chief Physician, to care for the Continental Army.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-27">27 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Continental Congress</b> resolved to raise two battalions of Marines.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-10">10 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
